🧠 Logical Address vs Physical Address

This is one of the most important OS concepts.

Imagine Process P1.

It thinks:

My code starts at address 0

But actual RAM location may be:

50000

Therefore:

Two types of addresses exist.

1. Logical Address (Virtual Address)

Generated by CPU.

Seen by process.

Example:

0
100
500
1000

Process believes:

I am starting from 0.
2. Physical Address

Actual RAM location.

Example:

50000
50100
50500
51000

Stored in real memory.

Easy Analogy

Your house address:

Flat 101

Postal department internally may use:

Block B
Sector 5
Area Code XYZ

User sees:

Flat 101

(Logical)

System uses:

Area Code XYZ

(Physical)

Address Translation

Question:

How does logical become physical?

Answer:

MMU

(Memory Management Unit)

What is MMU?

A hardware component inside/alongside the CPU that converts:

Logical Address
        ↓
Physical Address

Visualization:

CPU
 ↓
Logical Address
 ↓
MMU
 ↓
Physical Address
 ↓
RAM
Example

CPU generates:

100

MMU adds base value:

1000

Physical Address:

1100

RAM location:

1100

accessed.

Why Logical Addresses?

Without logical addresses:

Each program would need to know:

Exact RAM location.

Impossible.

Logical addresses provide:

Flexibility
Isolation
Protection
Address Binding

Another very important interview topic.

Question:

When is a program's address decided?

This is called:

Address Binding

There are 3 types.

1. Compile-Time Binding

Address fixed during compilation.

Example:

Start at address 5000

Compiler generates code accordingly.

Problem:

If location changes:

Recompile required.
2. Load-Time Binding

Address decided when program loads.

Loader chooses memory location.

More flexible.

3. Execution-Time Binding

Most modern systems use this.

Address decided while program runs.

MMU translates addresses dynamically.

Advantage:

Maximum flexibility
Dynamic Relocation

Suppose process moves:

RAM Location A
      ↓
RAM Location B

while running.

Can it continue?

Yes

because MMU changes mappings.

This is:

Dynamic Relocation
Why Modern OSes Love It?

Because processes can:

Move in memory
Grow dynamically
Share memory safely
Complete Flow
Program on SSD
        ↓
Loaded into RAM
        ↓
CPU generates Logical Address
        ↓
MMU converts to Physical Address
        ↓
RAM Access
